(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-11-01"] = {"3.1":{"s":"Compound angle formulae give the sine, cosine or tangent of the sum or the difference of two angles in terms of the sines, cosines and tangents of the angles themselves. They are proved first for the cosine of a difference, and all the others follow by changing the signs or by using the complementary angle. They are the base of all the later identities: double angles, factorisation and the angles of a triangle.","f":[{"x":"cos(A - B) = cos A*cos B + sin A*sin B","n":"Cosine of a difference. For a sum: cos(A + B) = cos A*cos B - sin A*sin B.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 3.1, p.36"},{"x":"sin(A - B) = sin A*cos B - cos A*sin B","n":"Sine of a difference. For a sum: sin(A + B) = sin A*cos B + cos A*sin B.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 3.1, p.36"},{"x":"tan(A + B) = (tan A + tan B)/(1 - tan A*tan B)","n":"Tangent of a sum. For a difference: tan(A - B) = (tan A - tan B)/(1 + tan A*tan B).","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 3.1, p.36"}],"w":["Cosine changes the sign between the terms: cos(A + B) has a minus, cos(A − B) has a plus. Sine keeps the sign.","In the tangent formulas the sign in the denominator is opposite to the sign in the numerator."],"x":["Compound angles are sums or differences of two angles, such as A + B and A − B. It is a very common mistake to think that sin(A + B) = sin A + sin B. This is false. The correct rule for a sum is sin(A + B) = sin A cos B + cos A sin B. For a difference, change the middle sign: sin(A − B) = sin A cos B − cos A sin B.","For cosines: cos(A + B) = cos A cos B − sin A sin B, and cos(A − B) = cos A cos B + sin A sin B. Notice that the sign in the middle is opposite for the cosine. For tangents: tan(A + B) = (tan A + tan B)/(1 − tan A tan B), and tan(A − B) = (tan A − tan B)/(1 + tan A tan B).","These formulas let us find the exact values of angles like 75° = 45° + 30°, 15° = 45° − 30° and 105° = 60° + 45°. For example, sin 75° = (√6 + √2)/4 = 0.9659 and cos 15° has the same value. Also tan 75° = 2 + √3 and tan 15° = 2 − √3.","Common mistakes: using sin(A + B) = sin A + sin B, and mixing the signs of the cosine formulas. A check: put A = B = 0 or A = 90°, B = 0 in the formula, and see if you get the right answer."],"e":"Find cos 105°. cos(60° + 45°) = cos 60° cos 45° − sin 60° sin 45° = (1/2)(√2/2) − (√3/2)(√2/2) = (√2 − √6)/4 = −0.2588. It is negative, since 105° is in the second quadrant."},"3.2":{"s":"Allied angles are angles that differ from a multiple of 90° by θ, such as 90° − θ, 180° + θ or 360° − θ. Their trigonometric functions can be written in terms of θ. Use two rules: decide whether the function changes to its co-function, which happens when the angle is an odd multiple of 90°, and decide the sign from the quadrant in which the angle falls.","f":[{"x":"sin(pi - theta) = sin(theta), cos(pi - theta) = -cos(theta)","n":"Second quadrant. Also tan(pi - theta) = -tan(theta).","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 3.2, p.40"},{"x":"sin(pi/2 - theta) = cos(theta), cos(pi/2 - theta) = sin(theta)","n":"Complementary angles. Also tan(pi/2 - theta) = cot(theta).","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 3.2, p.40"}],"w":["Sign comes from the quadrant of the original angle, as if θ were a small positive angle.","With 90° or 270° the function changes (sin to cos); with 180° or 360° it stays the same."],"x":["Allied angles are angles like π − θ, π + θ, 2π − θ and π/2 − θ, which are related to θ by multiples of a right angle. Their functions can be written in terms of the functions of θ. The result has a sign that depends on the quadrant, and the function changes to its co-function when the multiple of π/2 is odd.","Rules: sin(π − θ) = sin θ, cos(π − θ) = −cos θ, tan(π − θ) = −tan θ. sin(π + θ) = −sin θ, cos(π + θ) = −cos θ, tan(π + θ) = tan θ. sin(2π − θ) = −sin θ, cos(2π − θ) = cos θ, tan(2π − θ) = −tan θ. sin(π/2 − θ) = cos θ, cos(π/2 − θ) = sin θ, tan(π/2 − θ) = cot θ.","A simple method: (1) look at the quadrant of the angle for a small positive θ, and decide the sign using ASTC (all, sine, tangent, cosine are positive in the quadrants 1, 2, 3, 4). (2) If the multiple of π/2 is even (π, 2π), the function stays the same. If it is odd (π/2, 3π/2), sine changes to cosine, and so on.","Common mistake: forgetting the sign. For example, cos 120° = cos(180° − 60°) = −cos 60° = −1/2."],"e":"Find sin 150°: 150° = 180° − 30°, which is in the second quadrant, where sine is positive, so sin 150° = sin 30° = 1/2. Find tan 135° = tan(180° − 45°) = −tan 45° = −1."},"3.3":{"s":"The double angle formulae write the functions of 2θ in terms of θ. The sine is twice the product of sin θ and cos θ. The cosine has three equal forms, using cos² θ − sin² θ, 2 cos² θ − 1 or 1 − 2 sin² θ. The tangent is given by 2 tan θ over 1 − tan² θ. The cosine forms can be rearranged to get the half angle results.","f":[{"x":"sin(2*theta) = 2*sin(theta)*cos(theta) = 2*tan(theta)/(1 + tan(theta)^2)","n":"Sine of the double angle.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 3.3, p.43"},{"x":"cos(2*theta) = cos(theta)^2 - sin(theta)^2 = 2*cos(theta)^2 - 1 = 1 - 2*sin(theta)^2","n":"Cosine of the double angle; also (1 - tan(theta)^2)/(1 + tan(theta)^2).","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 3.3, p.43"},{"x":"tan(2*theta) = 2*tan(theta)/(1 - tan(theta)^2)","n":"Tangent of the double angle.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 3.3, p.43"}],"w":["cos 2θ has three forms: pick the one that suits the problem (only sin, only cos, or only tan).","sin 2θ is not 2 sin θ: the factor cos θ must be there."],"x":["Multiple angles are angles such as 2θ and 3θ. The double angle formulas come from putting A = B = θ in the compound angle formulas: sin 2θ = 2 sin θ cos θ = 2 tan θ/(1 + tan²θ). cos 2θ = cos²θ − sin²θ = 2cos²θ − 1 = 1 − 2sin²θ = (1 − tan²θ)/(1 + tan²θ). tan 2θ = 2 tan θ/(1 − tan²θ).","The cosine formulas can be solved for the half angle: sin²θ = (1 − cos 2θ)/2, and cos²θ = (1 + cos 2θ)/2. These are used in integration and in many proofs.","The triple angle formulas: sin 3θ = 3 sin θ − 4 sin³θ, cos 3θ = 4 cos³θ − 3 cos θ, and tan 3θ = (3 tan θ − tan³θ)/(1 − 3 tan²θ). A quick check: for θ = 30°, sin 90° = 3(1/2) − 4(1/8) = 1.","How to use them: if sin θ or tan θ is given, find the other ratios with a right triangle, and then use the formula. Common mistake: using sin 2θ = 2 sin θ. The angle is doubled, and the function is not."],"e":"If tan θ = 3/4, then sin 2θ = (3/2)/(1 + 9/16) = 24/25 = 0.96, and cos 2θ = (1 − 9/16)/(1 + 9/16) = 7/25 = 0.28. A check: sin²2θ + cos²2θ = (576 + 49)/625 = 1."},"3.4":{"s":"Factorisation formulae change a sum or a difference of two sines or cosines into a product, which often makes an expression easier to simplify. They come from the compound angle formulae by putting A = (C + D)/2 and B = (C − D)/2. The reverse formulae, which change a product into a sum or a difference, are called defactorisation formulae.","f":[{"x":"sin C + sin D = 2*sin((C + D)/2)*cos((C - D)/2)","n":"Sum of sines. Difference: sin C - sin D = 2*cos((C + D)/2)*sin((C - D)/2).","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 3.4, p.48"},{"x":"cos C + cos D = 2*cos((C + D)/2)*cos((C - D)/2)","n":"Sum of cosines. Difference: cos C - cos D = -2*sin((C + D)/2)*sin((C - D)/2).","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 3.4, p.48"}],"w":["cos C − cos D has a minus sign in front: −2 sin((C + D)/2) sin((C − D)/2).","Only a sum or difference of two sines or two cosines at a time can be factorised in this way."],"x":["Factorisation formulae change a sum or a difference of sines or cosines into a product. They come from adding and subtracting the compound angle formulas, with A = (C + D)/2 and B = (C − D)/2. sin C + sin D = 2 sin((C + D)/2) cos((C − D)/2). sin C − sin D = 2 cos((C + D)/2) sin((C − D)/2). cos C + cos D = 2 cos((C + D)/2) cos((C − D)/2). cos C − cos D = −2 sin((C + D)/2) sin((C − D)/2).","The reverse formulas change a product into a sum: 2 sin A cos B = sin(A + B) + sin(A − B), 2 cos A cos B = cos(A + B) + cos(A − B), and 2 sin A sin B = cos(A − B) − cos(A + B).","These are used to simplify expressions, to prove identities, and to find exact values: sin 75° + sin 15° = 2 sin 45° cos 30° = √6/2, and cos 75° − cos 15° = −2 sin 45° sin 30° = −√2/2. Also, cos 36° − cos 72° = 1/2, using cos 36° = (√5 + 1)/4 and cos 72° = (√5 − 1)/4, and sin 18° = (√5 − 1)/4.","Common mistake: forgetting the minus sign in the formula for cos C − cos D."],"e":"Prove that (sin 5θ + sin 3θ)/(cos 5θ + cos 3θ) = tan 4θ. The numerator is 2 sin 4θ cos θ. The denominator is 2 cos 4θ cos θ. The ratio is sin 4θ/cos 4θ = tan 4θ."},"3.5":{"s":"When A, B and C are the angles of a triangle, they add up to 180°, that is π radians. So A + B = π − C, and the functions of A + B turn into functions of C through the allied angle rules: sin(A + B) = sin C and cos(A + B) = −cos C. With the compound angle, double angle and factorisation formulae this gives many identities for the angles of a triangle.","w":["Use A + B + C = π first to turn a sum of two angles into the third angle.","cos(A + B) = −cos C: the sign changes for the cosine and the tangent, not for the sine."],"x":["In a triangle ABC, the three angles add up to π: A + B + C = π. So A + B = π − C, and the allied angle rules give relations between the angles. sin(A + B) = sin C. cos(A + B) = −cos C. tan(A + B) = −tan C. For half angles: (A + B)/2 = π/2 − C/2, so sin((A + B)/2) = cos(C/2), and cos((A + B)/2) = sin(C/2).","These relations are used to prove identities for the angles of a triangle. A famous one: in a triangle, tan A + tan B + tan C = tan A tan B tan C. The proof: tan(A + B) = −tan C gives (tan A + tan B)/(1 − tan A tan B) = −tan C, which can be rearranged to give the identity.","Another: in a triangle, sin 2A + sin 2B + sin 2C = 4 sin A sin B sin C. The proof uses the factorisation of sin 2A + sin 2B, and the fact that sin 2C = 2 sin C cos C, with cos C = −cos(A + B).","Common mistake: forgetting that A + B + C = π, which means that the three angles are not independent. Use C = π − A − B to remove one of them."],"e":"In a triangle, A = 60° and B = 45°. Then C = 75°, and sin C = sin(A + B) = sin 60° cos 45° + cos 60° sin 45° = (√6 + √2)/4 = 0.9659."}};
